./LOGBOOK.md:917:### 1208 — libssh2 adapter hits third-round channel/exec/window API boundary (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:919:- BLOCKER: `ssh2_channel_open` parses all RFC 4254 rejection reasons, then collapses them to `LIBSSH2_ERROR_CHANNEL_FAILURE`; no public typed/numeric reason survives. The remaining diagnostic string is not a stable control-flow API.
./LOGBOOK.md:921:- ROUTING: This is the third fork-gap round anticipated by TASK-260720-2sltje. Do not add a partial/mock-only adapter. Recommend failing libssh2 red at M0 unless its value justifies one explicitly approved consolidated fork extension for manual windows, typed open failures, and lossless exec-exit metadata; the alternative is an explicit M0-to-M3/common-contract revision. Exact evidence and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_third-public-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:923:### 1200 — Bounded libssh2 observation fork clears the remaining public-API clauses (TASK-260720-2sltje)
./LOGBOOK.md:924:- DECISION: The existing libssh2 patch remains a single allowlisted six-file delta. It adds typed server-initiated KEX `STARTED`/`SUCCEEDED`/`FAILED` observation with a monotonic generation and one bounded, nonblocking reply-correlated global-request operation; packet ordering, encryption, MAC, KEX algorithms, and crypto implementations remain unchanged.
./LOGBOOK.md:925:- DECISION: A timed-out global request stays outstanding until its late reply is drained. This deliberately prevents an unnumbered SSH `REQUEST_SUCCESS`/`REQUEST_FAILURE` from being miscorrelated with a newer request; callers can record a miss at `LIBSSH2_ERROR_TIMEOUT` and continue the same operation to restore the request slot.
./LOGBOOK.md:926:- VERIFICATION: Two clean source rebuilds produced byte-identical XCFramework locks; checksum/allowlist/tamper, public-symbol, notice, static/extension-safe, strict C warning, Swift packaging, 64-test core, and `swift build` gates pass. The local OpenSSH fixture observed 14 server-KEX transitions, including a deliberate failure, plus measured reply RTT and a deterministic timeout/miss.
./LOGBOOK.md:927:- ANOMALY: The broader pre-existing HEV Apple matrix stops before any Xcode build because `HevSocks5Tunnel.xcframework` contains macOS 10.14 and 11.0 object minima while its manifest requires only 10.14. This artifact is outside the libssh2 delta; the task-specific XCFramework inspection and required `make validate-core` gates pass.
./LOGBOOK.md:929:### 1145 — libssh2 adapter remains blocked on server-rekey observability and keepalive replies (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:930:- BLOCKER: The accepted `libssh2_session_rekey` fork clears client byte/time/manual trigger initiation only. The exact public header exports no KEX lifecycle callback or generation query. Pinned libssh2 handles inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` entirely inside private `packet.c`/`transport.c` state, so the adapter cannot truthfully emit `.serverInitiated`, mark `activeKeyExchange`, increment generation, or distinguish server-KEX success/failure through the public boundary.
./LOGBOOK.md:931:- BLOCKER: `libssh2_keepalive_send` writes `keepalive@libssh2.org` with `want_reply=1` but returns after queueing and stores no outstanding request or reply outcome. The public API exposes no generic reply-requiring global request or request-success/failure correlation. Consequently the adapter cannot measure reply RTT, enforce reply timeout/miss allowance, or distinguish acknowledgement from silence.
./LOGBOOK.md:932:- EVIDENCE: The public macOS XCFramework exports only `libssh2_session_rekey`, `libssh2_keepalive_config`, and `libssh2_keepalive_send` among relevant symbols. `keepalive.c:52-96` contains no reply wait/state; inbound server rekey is private at `packet.c:1336-1369` and `transport.c:407-425,1039-1048`. Trace callbacks are unavailable in non-debug builds and are not a supported protocol API.
./LOGBOOK.md:933:- ROUTING: Do not report these gates green or build a mock-only partial adapter. Recommend a separately reviewed minimal fork extension exposing typed server-KEX lifecycle plus a nonblocking reply-correlated global-request/keepalive API; alternatives are explicitly failing the libssh2 candidate or revising the mandatory contract. Exact options and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:935:### 1115 — Minimal libssh2 public client-rekey fork clears the adapter blocker (TASK-260720-3vwls7)
./LOGBOOK.md:936:- DECISION: Pinned libssh2 `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` carries one allowlisted two-file patch: `include/libssh2.h` publicly declares `libssh2_session_rekey`, and `src/session.c` adds a null-checked, nonblocking-friendly `BLOCK_ADJUST` wrapper over the existing `ssh2_kex_exchange(session, 1, &session->startup_key_state)`. No crypto, algorithm, packet, channel, or private-header surface changed.
./LOGBOOK.md:937:- SUPPLY CHAIN: The builder verifies libssh2 and OpenSSL 3.5.7 archive SHA-256 values before extraction or patch application, checks source-license hashes, rebuilds five static Apple architecture slices, emits the combined BSD-3-Clause/Apache-2.0/acknowledgement notice, and fails closed on patch/hash/path drift. Two clean rebuilds produced byte-identical XCFramework artifact locks.
./LOGBOOK.md:938:- VERIFICATION: The public-header-only C integration test drove the wrapper through three `EAGAIN` results, observed client-initiated NEWKEYS installation, then forced OpenSSH `RekeyLimit` server-initiated NEWKEYS and proved post-rekey channels remained usable. Fork verify/rekey/source-tamper gates, Swift public-module packaging test, native seam tests, strict Swift format, 62 core tests, and `swift build` pass; `ReluxTunnelCore` remains free of libssh2/OpenSSL imports.
./LOGBOOK.md:940:### 1030 — libssh2 adapter stops on absent public client-rekey API (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:941:- BLOCKER: Exact pinned archive `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` reproduced SHA-256 `744ba3e9…c87c`. Public `include/libssh2.h` has no rekey operation; initial `libssh2_session_handshake` calls private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=0)`, while only inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` reaches private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=1)` from `src/packet.c`.
./LOGBOOK.md:942:- IMPACT: Mandatory byte/time/explicit client rekey and `SSHTransport.requestRekey` cannot be implemented through the pinned public API. Calling the private symbol, exporting it in-task, replaying handshake, or reporting success without KEX would violate the task's no-patch/no-hidden-fallback boundary. Product code and partial packaging stopped before implementation.
./LOGBOOK.md:943:- ROUTING: Recommend recording libssh2 red/failed at this pin. The alternative is a separately authorized, audited libssh2 fork/public rekey hook (or a future upstream pin followed by full re-audit). Exact evidence and tradeoffs are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_libssh2-rekey-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:945:### 1020 — ReluxNIOSSH adapter blocked by TCP ownership and consumer-credit gaps (TASK-260715-1af33i)
./LOGBOOK.md:946:- BLOCKER: The neutral network seam injects an `SSHTCPConnection` with readiness/read/write/close only, while `NIOSSHHandler` can run only inside a NIO `Channel`. The seam exposes neither a transferable connected socket nor an engine-bootstrap capability. Opening a second socket ignores injection; adapter-private downcast breaks fake replaceability; a socketpair pump is a forced-fit extra transport.
./LOGBOOK.md:947:- BLOCKER: The fork debits on SSH-frame intake but returns the entire frame's credit immediately when `SSHChildChannel` fires it into the adapter pipeline. `SSHByteChannel.read(maximumBytes:)` may consume only a prefix, leaving an adapter-buffered suffix after the fork has already advertised credit. The fork also exposes no local maximum-packet/intake option, so `maximumBufferedReadBytes` cannot remain an independent hard bound.
./LOGBOOK.md:949:- ROUTING: Recommend an accepted candidate-neutral connected-socket/engine-bootstrap seam plus ReluxNIOSSH consumer-driven receive-credit and maximum-packet/intake hooks, each with deterministic tests. Exact evidence, rejected workarounds, alternatives, and resume condition are attached as `TASK-260715-1af33i_adapter-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:953:- FINDING: External-signer coverage is complete for upstream's signature universe: `NIOSSHSignature.BackingSignature` has exactly ed25519/P256/P384/P521, and the fork's external-signature conversion (`UserAuthenticationMethod.swift:339`) covers all four. The `preconditionFailure` in the sync message init is unreachable from public API — the async `make` path is the only caller routing `.externalPublicKey`.
./LOGBOOK.md:954:- FINDING: Snapshot lifecycle is sticky-correct: `SSHConnectionStateMachine`'s own snapshot resets to nil on transition to `userAuthentication`/`active`, but `NIOSSHHandler` re-reads it after every processed message while the KEX state machine still holds `NegotiationResult`, and only overwrites on non-nil (`NIOSSHHandler.swift:684`). Consequence: during a rekey the handler snapshot updates when the new `NegotiationResult` is computed (KEXINIT processing), slightly before newKeys activate.
./LOGBOOK.md:955:- NOTE: Adapter caveats: (1) for AEAD ciphers (`aes*-gcm@openssh.com`) `macAlgorithm` reports the negotiated-but-unused MAC-list value — standard SSH semantics, do not treat it as the wire MAC; (2) `.global` promise resolves `.failure` as success-with-value (RTT measurable on refusal — correct for `keepalive@openssh.com` liveness), only connection errors fail the promise; (3) untested-but-documented paths: `.success` reply payload for `.global` requests and empty-allowlist fail-closed negotiation — acceptable residual risk, worth covering when the adapter integrates.
./LOGBOOK.md:957:### 1003 — ReluxNIOSSH fork exposes adapter-conformance seams (TASK-260720-34d4du)
./LOGBOOK.md:958:- IMPLEMENTATION: Public-key auth now accepts SSH wire-format public key bytes plus an event-loop future signer over NIOSSH's exact RFC 4252 payload; the offer stores no `NIOSSHPrivateKey`. Ed25519 and ECDSA raw signatures are converted inside the fork before the existing serializer and verifier paths.
./LOGBOOK.md:960:- IMPLEMENTATION: Client KEX/host-key allowlists filter only bundled upstream implementations and fail closed when empty/unsupported. `NIOSSHHandler.negotiatedAlgorithmsSnapshot` copies exact KEX, host-key, cipher, and MAC strings from `NegotiationResult`; it never echoes configured lists.
./LOGBOOK.md:964:### 0935 — ReluxNIOSSH adapter blocked by three missing public fork seams (TASK-260715-1af33i)
./LOGBOOK.md:965:- BLOCKER: The neutral credential is an opaque async signer (`SSHPublicKeyCredential`), but ReluxNIOSSH public-key auth accepts only concrete `NIOSSHPrivateKey` and signs synchronously inside an internal message initializer. The adapter cannot authenticate without a candidate downcast/private-key export or a new external-signer fork API.
./LOGBOOK.md:966:- BLOCKER: ReluxNIOSSH exposes only TCP-forwarding as a public global request. Its generic reply-requiring request sender and message type are internal, so the adapter cannot send/measure a supported `keepalive@openssh.com` request. Substituting remote TCP forwarding or writing encrypted packets beside the engine is invalid.
./LOGBOOK.md:967:- BLOCKER: `SSHClientConfiguration` exposes transport-protection schemes but not caller KEX/host-key allowlists or a public exact negotiated KEX/host-key/cipher/MAC result; the only negotiation result/test accessor is internal. Inferring results from capabilities would violate E-ALGO.
./LOGBOOK.md:971:- IMPLEMENTATION: Added `relay-portable-assets` / `inspect-assets`, which requires a caller-owned total bundle budget and emits a path-free four-asset report only after exact layout/name, regular executable mode, raw format/machine, Go CPU metadata, linkage, minimum runtime, dynamic-library, debug-section, size, SHA-256, and privacy checks. An over-budget report is retained before the command fails; no default budget silently creates product policy.
./LOGBOOK.md:972:- VERIFICATION: A detached clean worktree at `58676a23e2e0fb3fcc1b5005d59c6ed56d3c0096` built exactly Darwin/Linux amd64/arm64 with pinned Go 1.26.5, `--require-clean`, release linker flags, protocol v1 identity `0.1.0`, and source epoch `1784656987`. The four files total 10,259,950 bytes and are retained in task outcome archive `TASK-260715-24icoz_portable-relay-assets.tar.gz`; each is mode 0755, stripped with no DWARF companion, and passed format/linkage/metadata/privacy inspection.
./LOGBOOK.md:975:- DECISION NEEDED: Supply the approved total bundle budget in bytes, restore an approved native Linux fixture (the declared GitHub Actions rows after billing is fixed), and supply native Intel execution or explicitly approve Rosetta as the darwin/amd64 baseline. Until those inputs exist, AC3/AC4 cannot be claimed and the task remains blocked rather than treating cross-build or a one-byte diagnostic budget as release evidence.
